# Source Analysis Toolchain

## Operating Rule

Pick the tool by the evidence needed, not by novelty.  Start with the cheapest
tool that can falsify the claim, then climb only when it leaves an important
unknown.

When a specific command recipe is needed, read
`references/tool-guide.md`.  It is the compact manual-backed guide for the
requested tools, with local paths and package notes where they affect use.
Read `references/reactos-gcc.md` for RosBE MinGW GCC builds, ReactOS PE
symbolization, and host-versus-guest instrumentation boundaries.

## Tool Routing

| Need | First tools | Escalate when |
|---|---|---|
| Locate files, tokens, names, config, docs | `rg`, `git grep`/`git-grep`, `rg --files` | text hits do not prove call/data flow |
| Symbols and cross references | `ctags`/`readtags`, `gtags`/`global`, `cscope` | macro expansion, generated code, or compile flags matter |
| Call graph or control sketch | `cflow`, `cscope`, `global`, `joern` | the flow is type-sensitive, indirect, or security-critical |
| Syntax or API shape | `ast-grep`, `tree-sitter`, `weggli`, `comby` | the rewrite must be semantic or cross-file |
| C/C++ defects | compiler Werror, `cppcheck`, `sparse`, `smatch`, `infer`, `codeql` | the warning needs whole-program dataflow or a custom query |
| Semantic patching | `spatch` | the match must preserve C syntax across many files |
| Metrics and scope sizing | `lizard`, `scc` | metrics contradict code ownership or compile inclusion |
| Binary and ABI proof | `readelf`, `nm`, `objdump`, `addr2line`, `gdb` | runtime loading or stripped symbols obscure the path |
| RE and binary diffing | `radare2`/`r2`, `radiff2`, `rizin`, Ghidra headless, `LIEF`, `binwalk` | source and symbols are absent or untrusted |
| Runtime syscall/library path | `strace`, `ltrace`, `gdb`, `python-ptrace`, `Frida` | kernel, loader, privilege, or timing is the mechanism |
| Kernel/user tracing | `bpftrace`, BCC tools (`bcc-tools`), `trace-cmd`, LTTng, SystemTap, Perfetto | the failure spans processes, threads, or kernel events |
| Profiling and memory cost | `perf` with `hotspot`, `sysprof`, `heaptrack`, `valgrind` with `callgrind_annotate` | cost must be attributed to stack, allocation, or timeline |
| Fuzzing and testcase mutation | AFL++, honggfuzz, radamsa | the oracle or harness is not yet stable |

## Evidence Discipline

- Cite how a fact was found: command plus query, not only file and line.
- On Arch or CachyOS hosts, inventory before judging availability: use
  `command -v` for paths, `readlink -f` for wrappers, `pacman -Qo` for
  ownership, `pacman -Ss` for sync repositories, and `paru -Ss` for mixed
  repo/AUR visibility. Use `paru -S --needed` when the task requires a missing
  tool, prefer repo packages over AUR when routes are equivalent, prefer
  Chaotic-AUR over plain AUR, and record package source plus executable path
  when the result becomes durable evidence.
- Record launch-name traps explicitly: `/usr/bin/rg` can be shadowed by Codex,
  Chipsec launches as `chipsec-main`/`chipsec-util`, Intel XED launches as
  `intel-xed`, the preferred Vostro XED route is `intel-sde` plus
  `xed-intel` instead of `intel-xed-git`, and Vostro compiler evidence uses
  system Clang 22 rather than unowned `clang-21` wrappers.
- For Ghidra automation, use the headless entrypoint first.  Resolve and record
  the launcher and support binary, usually `/usr/bin/ghidra-analyzeHeadless`
  and `/opt/ghidra/support/analyzeHeadless`, because bare `analyzeHeadless` may
  not be on `PATH` even when `/usr/bin/ghidra` exists.  The headless command
  has mandatory positional project arguments:
  `ghidra-analyzeHeadless <project_location> <project_name> -import <binary>`.
  Create `project_location` before launch.  Ghidra creates the project files
  inside an existing parent directory, but reports `Directory not found` when
  that parent does not exist.
  Do not pass `-v` for version discovery; this launcher parses it as the
  project location/name and reports that the project is invalid.  Use
  `pacman -Q ghidra`, package ownership, or launcher help output for version
  and provenance checks.  Treat GUI state as attended context only after a
  headless project and logs exist.
- Record package-to-command mismatches.  Examples on this host class include
  `libdisasm` -> `x86dis`, `udis86` -> `udcli`, `zydis` ->
  `ZydisDisasm`/`ZydisInfo`, `capstone` -> `cstool`, `python-distorm` ->
  `python -m distorm3`, and `ghidra` -> `ghidra-analyzeHeadless`.  For
  Windows and ReactOS artifacts, also record `regrippy` -> `regrip` and
  `reg-*`, `regipy` -> `registry-*`, `regripper` -> `regripper`, `winregfs`
  -> `mount.winregfs`/`fsck.winregfs`, `libvmdk-python` ->
  `vmdkinfo`/`vmdkmount`, `libvshadow` -> `vshadowinfo`/`vshadowmount`,
  `wimlib` -> `wiminfo`/`wimextract`/`wimlib-imagex`, `msitools` ->
  `msiinfo`/`msidump`/`msiextract`, and `lessmsi` ->
  `lessmsi`/`lessmsi-gui`.
- Prefer configured binary repositories before AUR when installing tools:
  CachyOS or Arch repositories first, Chaotic-AUR next, BlackArch for RE and
  forensic packages, and AUR only when none of those repositories provide the
  needed package. Record exceptions in the evidence bundle.
- Prefer repo-local wrappers and `compile_commands.json` when C/C++ tools need
  real build flags.
- Keep generated indexes and traces out of commits unless the repo explicitly
  treats them as retained evidence.
- For tracing, profiling, fuzzing, and hardware-facing work, record the command,
  kernel/user permissions, target PID or binary, and stop condition.
- For GPU work, check `dmesg` and loaded module or ICD paths before drawing
  driver conclusions.

## Done Means

The result states the path proved, the command that proved it, the tool limits,
and the next stronger probe if the current evidence is insufficient.

