   675	     When $\phi: M \to G$ is the {\em versal embedding}
   676	     of $M$, then, by the definition of Huneke-Eisenbud-Ulrich,
   677	     the {\em Rees ideal of M} is the kernel of $Sym(\phi)$. Thus the
   678	     Rees Algebra of M is the image of $Sym(\phi)$.
   679	     
   680	     In most cases the kernel of the 
   681	     $Sym(\phi)$ is the same for any embedding $\phi$ of
   682	     $M$ into a free module:
   683	     
   684	     {\bf Theorem (Eisenbud-Huneke-Ulrich, Thms 0.2 and 1.4):} Let R be a Noetherian ring
   685	     and let $M$ be a finitely generated R-module. Let $\phi: M \to G$
   686	     be a versal map of $M$ to a free module. Assume that $\phi$ is an inclusion, and let
   687	     $\psi: M \to G'$ be any inclusion. If $R$ is torsion-free over $\ZZ$
   688	     or $R$ is unmixed and generically Gorenstein, or $M$ is free locally
   689	     at each associated prime of $R$, or $G=R$, then the kernel of $Sym(\phi)$ and the
   690	     kernel of $Sym(\psi)$ are equal.
   691	          
   692	     It follows that in the good cases above the Rees 
   693	     ideal is equal to the saturation
   694	     of the defining ideal of symmetric 
   695	     algebra of $M$ with respect to any
   696	     element f of the ground ring such 
   697	     that $M[f^{-1}]$ is free, or is simply {\em of linear type},
   698	     meaning that $Sym(\phi)$ is a monomorphism. This is the case,
   699	     for example, when M is an ideal and $M[f^{-1}]$ is generated
   700	     by a regular sequence.
   701	     This fact often leads to 
   702	     a faster computation than computing the
   703	     kernel of $Sym(\phi)$ directly.
   704	       
   705	     Here is an example of the pathological case of
